Igbo AI began by examining the existing ecosystem. Igbo language technology already has valuable datasets, lexical resources, trained systems and research communities. Our responsibility is to understand this work, credit it accurately and determine where responsible reuse can support new research.

The initial review covered speech and text datasets, ASR, TTS, Igbo NLP representations, language models, dialect and pronunciation resources, tone and diacritic research, licensing and African NLP communities. Its purpose was to identify useful foundations and missing infrastructure. This is a landscape review, not a completed dataset provenance audit or an independent reproduction of model results.

## Lexical and linguistic foundations

Nkọwa okwu / IgboAPI connects dictionary data with dialect variants, example sentences and pronunciation recordings. It is especially relevant to the questions behind IgboPronounce and dialect-aware speech. We will distinguish software access from the rights attached to dictionary material and recordings before selecting any training input.

IgboBERT and IgboBERTa demonstrate prior work in Igbo text representations and NLP. Such work can inform future investigations into normalisation, diacritics and language understanding. We do not assume that named entity recognition results establish performance on these other tasks.

## Speech data: useful resources, different conditions

Google WAXAL includes high-quality Igbo TTS material. The current dataset card lists the Media Trust Igbo subset under CC BY-SA 4.0; licences vary between providers. That makes release-specific review essential, even when a collection is described broadly as open.

Mozilla Common Voice Igbo provides community speech relevant to ASR, speaker variation and robustness. The reviewed release lists CC0 together with platform conditions against speaker identification and re-sharing. Google FLEURS offers an Igbo-inclusive multilingual benchmark useful for comparative ASR research. Evaluation splits must remain separate from training data.

African Voices, operated by Data Science Nigeria, is a potentially significant Nigerian-language speech resource. Before deciding how it can be used, we need to reconcile dataset-specific rights, platform terms, version metadata and contributor provenance. An initial licensing review is not permission to ingest or redistribute a collection.

## Existing systems deserve careful comparison

IkengaTTS is an important recent Igbo TTS reference. It demonstrates that meaningful Igbo synthesis development is already happening. We have not reproduced its reported results. Our proposed work investigates explicit tone and phoneme supervision, dialect control, native-speaker evaluation and permissive infrastructure through controlled comparisons.

Meta MMS provides multilingual speech research baselines. Its TTS licence includes non-commercial restrictions, and exact task and checkpoint coverage must be checked. It is therefore a possible reference baseline, rather than an assumed permanent foundation for a permissively released Igbo system.

NCAIR and Awarri publish Igbo ASR and multilingual language work, including N-ATLaS. These initiatives show a growing Nigerian-language AI ecosystem. Their release terms and underlying model conditions require individual review. Listing them here does not indicate a collaboration or independent endorsement of performance claims.

## Research in a wider community

Masakhane is an important African NLP community spanning translation, named entity recognition, classification, question answering and evaluation. Igbo AI intends to engage with this ecosystem respectfully. Collaboration should begin with listening, attribution and clearly agreed contributions. None of the external projects in this review are being announced as Igbo AI partners.

## The infrastructure gap

Our initial landscape review did not identify a single mature open project bringing pronunciation, tone, phonetics, dialects, verified speech, TTS, ASR, native-speaker evaluation, tooling and responsible provenance together. This is a bounded finding from an initial review, not an absolute claim that no such effort exists. We welcome corrections and additional references.

The proposed response is a connected foundation: IgboPronounce, IgboTone and IgboPhonemizer; a carefully verified IgboSpeech Gold collection; IgboSpeechBench; dialect-conditioned TTS and permissive ASR; and community recording and evaluation infrastructure. Later language intelligence can build on this work. These are planned outputs, not existing releases.

## The first research question

Can explicit tone, phoneme and dialect supervision make an Igbo speech system measurably more native-sounding than existing character-based Igbo TTS and general-purpose AI voices?

This remains a question. A useful study would hold data and evaluation conditions as consistent as possible, compare linguistic supervision choices, include names and code-switching, and combine automated metrics with blind native-speaker listening. We have not run that benchmark or demonstrated superiority.

## What happens next

The initial technology landscape, dataset/model inventory, licensing landscape and major-gap identification are complete. Phase 0 remains In Progress: potential collaborator mapping is underway, while a formal provenance audit, academic and community consultation and an initial benchmark specification remain planned.

The linked Landscape page is a living register maintained through the Research Desk. Each reference records its review date, access considerations and provisional role. Corrections, missing resources and research enquiries are welcome at support@igbo.ai. No external data or model weights have been incorporated into an Igbo AI release by publishing this review.','Igbo AI · Galaxyway AI','Published','2026-09-05T15:13:05.073Z','An initial review of Igbo speech data, NLP, TTS, ASR, licensing and the open linguistic infrastructure still needed.');
